{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.104,
            0.475,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "PAIR OF LINEAR EQUATIONS IN TWO VARIABLES"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.873,
            0.104,
            0.899,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "25"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.139,
            0.335,
            0.158
          ],
          "content": "Let us try this approach."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.164,
            0.9,
            0.204
          ],
          "content": "Denote the number of rides that Akhila had by x, and the number of times she played Hoopla by y. Now the situation can be represented by the two equations:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.45,
            0.211,
            0.898,
            0.25
          ],
          "content": "y = \\frac{1}{2}x\\tag{1}",
          "image_source": "images/page_1_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.392,
            0.255,
            0.898,
            0.274
          ],
          "content": "3x + 4y = 20\\tag{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_1_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.281,
            0.902,
            0.32
          ],
          "content": "Can we find the solutions of this pair of equations? There are several ways of finding these, which we will study in this chapter."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.332,
            0.779,
            0.353
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "3.2 Graphical Method of Solution of a Pair of Linear Equations"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.359,
            0.902,
            0.481
          ],
          "content": "A pair of linear equations which has no solution, is called an inconsistent pair of linear equations. A pair of linear equations in two variables, which has a solution, is called a consistent pair of linear equations. A pair of linear equations which are equivalent has infinitely many distinct common solutions. Such a pair is called a dependent pair of linear equations in two variables. Note that a dependent pair of linear equations is always consistent."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.485,
            0.9,
            0.525
          ],
          "content": "We can now summarise the behaviour of lines representing a pair of linear equations in two variables and the existence of solutions as follows:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.165,
            0.534,
            0.9,
            0.573
          ],
          "content": "(i) the lines may intersect in a single point. In this case, the pair of equations has a unique solution (consistent pair of equations)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.581,
            0.9,
            0.619
          ],
          "content": "(ii) the lines may be parallel. In this case, the equations have no solution (inconsistent pair of equations)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.627,
            0.9,
            0.666
          ],
          "content": "(iii) the lines may be coincident. In this case, the equations have infinitely many solutions [dependent (consistent) pair of equations]."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.671,
            0.602,
            0.689
          ],
          "content": "Consider the following three pairs of equations."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.165,
            0.693,
            0.775,
            0.712
          ],
          "content": "(i) $x - 2y = 0$ and $3x + 4y - 20 = 0$ (The lines intersect)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.716,
            0.772,
            0.735
          ],
          "content": "(ii) $2x + 3y - 9 = 0$ and $4x + 6y - 18 = 0$ (The lines coincide)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.739,
            0.799,
            0.759
          ],
          "content": "(iii) $x + 2y - 4 = 0$ and $2x + 4y - 12 = 0$ (The lines are parallel)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.77,
            0.901,
            0.856
          ],
          "content": "Let us now write down, and compare, the values of $\\frac { a _ { 1 } } { a _ { 2 } } , \\frac { b _ { 1 } } { b _ { 2 } }$ and $\\frac { c _ { 1 } } { c _ { 2 } }$ in all the three examples. Here, $a _ { \\mathrm { 1 } } , \\; b _ { \\mathrm { 1 } } , \\; c _ { \\mathrm { 1 } }$ and $a _ { _ 2 } , \\; b _ { _ 2 } , \\; c _ { _ 2 }$ denote the coefficents of equations given in the general form in Section 3.2."
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.967,
            0.563,
            0.982
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
